WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:02.650
Last term, my class trained a

2
00:00:02.650 --> 00:00:04.430
model to spot sick bean leaves.

3
00:00:05.110 --> 00:00:07.190
It called almost every leaf

4
00:00:07.190 --> 00:00:09.090
healthy, even the sick ones!

5
00:00:09.790 --> 00:00:11.490
The computer wasn’t the problem.

6
00:00:12.120 --> 00:00:13.140
Our data was.

7
00:00:14.510 --> 00:00:17.870
Today: data first, because a model can

8
00:00:17.870 --> 00:00:20.210
only learn what its examples show it.

9
00:00:21.290 --> 00:00:23.410
A model never sees the garden.

10
00:00:24.120 --> 00:00:26.560
All it knows comes from your examples.

11
00:00:27.500 --> 00:00:29.420
Each one is something you collected,

12
00:00:29.860 --> 00:00:33.080
like a leaf photo, plus a label: the

13
00:00:33.080 --> 00:00:35.240
right answer, “healthy” or “sick”.

14
00:00:35.710 --> 00:00:37.130
Together, they’re your data.

15
00:00:38.420 --> 00:00:41.140
So plan before you collect anything.

16
00:00:41.930 --> 00:00:43.050
What will you collect:

17
00:00:43.510 --> 00:00:44.650
photos of bean leaves?

18
00:00:45.350 --> 00:00:47.890
Which labels: healthy and sick?

19
00:00:48.530 --> 00:00:51.970
How many: at least ten of each, to start?

20
00:00:52.640 --> 00:00:55.700
And how varied: young and old leaves,

21
00:00:56.060 --> 00:00:58.640
sun and shade, different phones?

22
00:01:00.040 --> 00:01:01.500
Then check for three problems.

23
00:01:02.260 --> 00:01:05.220
Wrong labels: a sick leaf filed as

24
00:01:05.220 --> 00:01:07.320
healthy teaches a wrong answer.

25
00:01:07.950 --> 00:01:11.530
Too few examples: five photos can’t

26
00:01:11.530 --> 00:01:13.150
show every way a leaf looks.

27
00:01:13.860 --> 00:01:16.260
And one kind far outnumbering

28
00:01:16.260 --> 00:01:19.240
another: ninety healthy leaves and

29
00:01:19.240 --> 00:01:21.800
ten sick, like Mr Joseph’s class.

30
00:01:23.140 --> 00:01:25.280
A dataset is all the labelled

31
00:01:25.280 --> 00:01:26.880
examples a model learns from.

32
00:01:27.510 --> 00:01:29.410
A good one has correct labels,

33
00:01:29.910 --> 00:01:32.410
enough of each kind, and a fair mix

34
00:01:32.410 --> 00:01:33.750
of everything it will meet.

35
00:01:34.970 --> 00:01:36.410
Think of your data as the

36
00:01:36.410 --> 00:01:37.830
model’s only textbook.

37
00:01:38.480 --> 00:01:40.480
If answers in the back are wrong,

38
00:01:40.900 --> 00:01:42.120
it learns wrong answers.

39
00:01:42.950 --> 00:01:45.590
If there’s one page on fractions, it won’t

40
00:01:45.590 --> 00:01:47.130
be ready for fractions in the exam.

41
00:01:48.430 --> 00:01:49.010
Quick check!

42
00:01:49.800 --> 00:01:52.900
Your class photographs forty ripe

43
00:01:52.900 --> 00:01:56.520
and forty unripe tomatoes, all at

44
00:01:56.520 --> 00:01:58.080
noon in bright sun.

45
00:01:58.660 --> 00:01:59.420
What’s missing?

46
00:02:00.780 --> 00:02:02.320
Photos in other light.

47
00:02:03.010 --> 00:02:05.950
The model never saw shade, dusk

48
00:02:05.950 --> 00:02:07.570
or a dim market stall.

49
00:02:08.200 --> 00:02:10.720
It may fail the first time it meets one.

50
00:02:11.330 --> 00:02:14.230
Eighty is a good start, but variety

51
00:02:14.230 --> 00:02:15.710
matters as much as numbers.

52
00:02:17.110 --> 00:02:18.370
Collect fairly, too.

53
00:02:19.060 --> 00:02:19.760
Recording voices?

54
00:02:20.520 --> 00:02:22.500
Ask each person first, and

55
00:02:22.500 --> 00:02:23.300
say what it’s for.

56
00:02:23.980 --> 00:02:25.240
Photographing handwriting?

57
00:02:26.120 --> 00:02:27.620
Use a made-up sentence,

58
00:02:28.160 --> 00:02:29.380
never names or addresses.

59
00:02:30.140 --> 00:02:31.680
Taking photos at the market?

60
00:02:32.370 --> 00:02:34.650
Photograph the fruit, not the

61
00:02:34.650 --> 00:02:36.230
sellers, unless they agree.

62
00:02:37.500 --> 00:02:39.240
In the app, you’ll teach a

63
00:02:39.240 --> 00:02:40.660
small network to see.

64
00:02:41.250 --> 00:02:44.070
Draw a circle, a cross or a line,

65
00:02:44.430 --> 00:02:45.670
and add it to its group.

66
00:02:46.280 --> 00:02:49.460
Press Train, then test a new drawing.

67
00:02:50.110 --> 00:02:52.910
Then remove examples, or add a

68
00:02:52.910 --> 00:02:54.890
messy one, and see what changes.

69
00:02:56.220 --> 00:02:56.980
Your turn.

70
00:02:57.600 --> 00:03:00.240
You want to train a model on your

71
00:03:00.240 --> 00:03:01.380
classmates’ handwriting.

72
00:03:02.180 --> 00:03:03.180
What should you do first?

73
00:03:04.570 --> 00:03:07.030
Ask each one first, and keep

74
00:03:07.030 --> 00:03:08.370
names off the pages.

75
00:03:09.120 --> 00:03:11.820
Remember: plan what to collect, the

76
00:03:11.820 --> 00:03:14.360
labels, how many and how varied.

77
00:03:15.030 --> 00:03:17.770
Check for wrong labels, too few examples

78
00:03:17.770 --> 00:03:20.270
and one kind outnumbering another.

79
00:03:20.950 --> 00:03:23.650
A model is only as good as its data.

80
00:03:24.930 --> 00:03:27.910
Try it yourself: collect twenty labelled

81
00:03:27.910 --> 00:03:30.410
examples of something around you: leaves,

82
00:03:30.930 --> 00:03:32.810
fruit at the market, or handwriting.

83
00:03:33.630 --> 00:03:34.670
Count each label.

84
00:03:35.380 --> 00:03:38.520
Then write down what’s missing: other

85
00:03:38.520 --> 00:03:41.300
kinds, other light, other places.
